The reason is that Visual C++ Redistributables are runtime dependencies used by applications and services. CyberArk installation/upgrade material specifically installs or validates Visual C++ packages for PSM, including both x86 and x64 packages in newer releases.

The risk depends heavily on what your cleanup team means by “cleanup”:

Activity	Risk	Assessment
Update current VC++ v14 / 2015-2022/2026 runtime to a newer supported v14 version	Low–Moderate	Generally safe
Patch x86 and x64 redistributables to current supported versions	Low–Moderate	Recommended with testing
Remove an older v14 build after installing the newer v14	Low	Normally handled automatically by the installer
Remove VC++ 2013/2012/2010 because vulnerability tooling calls them obsolete	Moderate–High	Do not do without dependency validation
Remove x86 because Windows is 64-bit	High	Not recommended; 32-bit CyberArk/connection components may depend on it

Microsoft confirms that Visual C++ 2015 and later v14 runtimes are cumulative and binary-compatible. A newer v14 runtime replaces the older v14 runtime and is designed to support applications compiled against previous v14 releases.

The important exception is Visual C++ 2013 and earlier. Those runtimes are side-by-side packages and are not covered by the v14 binary-compatibility guarantee. Microsoft specifically recommends determining which applications are actually using legacy runtimes before removing them.
